e deeper children are removable? Possibly they are non-required and removable, but they have their own retained children? That leads to recursion. Eventually, the deepest leaves are removable. In a single pass, we process leaves first. Suppose we have node A (depth 2, removable, not required) with child B (depth 3, removable, not required) and B has no children. Processing depth descending: B (depth 3) is processed first; it has no children, so can be removed. Then A (depth 2) is processed; it has child B, but B has been removed, so A has no retained children. So A can be removed. So that works fine.\n\nNow consider a case: node A (depth 2) with child B (depth 3), and B has child C (depth 4). All are removable and not required. Process order: C (depth4), then B (depth3), then A (depth2). C removed first, then B sees no retained children (C removed), B removed, then A sees no retained children, A removed. So all removed. Good.\n\nNow consider a case where B is non-removable or required, e.g., B is required. Then B's child C may be processed.. B is required so we never try to remove it. When processing A, it has child B which is retained (since required), so A cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nNow consider a case where B is removable and not required, but B has a child C that is required (so C cannot be removed). When processing C (required), we skip (or not in candidate list because symbol required). Then B is processed; B has a child C which is retained (required), so B cannot be removed. Then A is processed; A has child B which is retained, so A cannot be removed. So all retained except maybe other branches. So that seems correct.\n\nNow consider a case where B is removable but fails the required symbol check because its removal would remove the last instance of a required symbol (but B's symbol is not required, but its subtree might contain a required symbol? Actually, if B has a child C that is required, we already said C is retained, so B cannot be removed. So the only case where a node could be removed even though its subtree contains required symbols is if the required symbol is on B itself. But B's symbol is not required, so no issue. So the only required symbols are in nodes. So the check about required symbols after removal only concerns the node's own symbol if it is required. Since we filter out required symbol nodes from candidates, the removal of a non-required node never affects required symbol counts. Therefore, the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied for non-required nodes, provided that no required node is in its subtree. But we prevent that by the child retention check: if any child is retained, and required nodes are always retained, then any required child prevents removal. So the algorithm simplifies to: for each candidate (removable, not required) in depth-descending order, if it has no retained children, remove it.\n\nThus the algorithm is straightforward.\n\nBut wait: what about a node that is not required but has a descendant that is required through multiple levels? For example, node A (depth1, removable) -> B (depth2, removable) -> C (depth3, required). C is retained because required. B has a retained child (C), so B cannot be removed. A has a retained child (B), so A cannot be removed. So fine.\n\nHence, the algorithm can be: maintain set of retained IDs. Initially all IDs. Sort candidate nodes as described. For each candidate in order, if all its children have been removed (i.e., no child ID in retained set), then remove the node (remove ID from retained, append to removed list). That's it.\n\nWe don't need to track required symbol counts because the removal of non-required nodes cannot remove required symbols (since required nodes can't be removed and their ancestors can't be removed if they have required descendants). So the only way a required symbol could be removed is if the node itself has a required symbol, but we filter those from candidates.