ther test cases, similar.\n\nWe should also consider nodes that have children that are not in retained set because they were removed earlier. That's fine.\n\nNow implement solve(case). We'll parse case as a dict.\n\nWe need to ensure we handle case where node has no parent (root). children list building: for each node, if parent is not None (or null string?), the parent field is string or null. In data, root has parent null. So we can treat parent key as None. For building children mapping, we can initialize a dict with empty list for each id, then for each node with parent not None, append node['id'] to children[parent].\n\nNow, we must not mutate case, but we can read it.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Extract nodes list: case['nodes']\n- Extract required_symbols set: set(case['required_symbols'])\n- Build node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n- Build children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes} (or use defaultdict)\n- For each node in nodes:\n    if node['parent'] is not None:\n        children[node['parent']].append(node['id'])\n- retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n- candidates = [node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols]\n- Sort candidates: sorted(candidates, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- removed = []\n- for node in candidates:\n    if node['id'] not in retained: continue (shouldn't happen)\n    # check if any child in retained\n    has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children[node['id']])\n    if not has_retained_child:\n        retained.remove(node['id'])\n        removed.append(node['id'])\n- retained_sorted = sorted(retained) # alphabetical string sort\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut note: children list might include children that are not in retained (removed). That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: what about nodes that are removable but have no children? Then has_retained_child = False, so they can b